-- functions.spl — LLM function definitions for Recipe 89: arXiv Digest (EAAI-27 workshop)
-- Imported by summarize_arxiv_paper.spl via IMPORT 'functions.spl'.
--
-- Two-step map-reduce per paper, matching the EAAI-27 workshop capstone spec
-- (docs/conference/EAAI-2027/.../handout.md, "Capstone Demo: arXiv Paper
-- Summarization"): one summary per section (map), then one structured
-- per-paper digest — a paragraph per section plus a 3-bullet contribution
-- summary (reduce).

CREATE FUNCTION section_summarizer(section_title TEXT, section_text TEXT, max_tokens INT)
RETURNS TEXT
AS $$
Summarize the research paper section below in exactly 2-3 complete sentences.
Rules (violations cause downstream truncation — follow them strictly):
- NO preamble. Do not start with "Here is", "This section", "The following", or anything other than the actual content.
- NO bullet points, headings, or lists.
- 2-3 sentences maximum. Stop after the third sentence even if there is more to say.
- Plain prose only. Focus on the key method or finding.

Section: {section_title}

{section_text}
$$;

CREATE FUNCTION group_summarizer(group_summaries TEXT, max_tokens INT)
RETURNS TEXT
AS $$
Compress the section summaries below into one short Markdown block.
Rules (follow strictly):
- Start with a "### " header using the first section title in the input.
- Write exactly 3-4 complete sentences covering the key methods and findings across ALL input sections.
- NO preamble, NO bullet points, NO additional headers.
- Stop after the fourth sentence.

Section summaries:
{group_summaries}
$$;

CREATE FUNCTION paper_digest_writer(title TEXT, section_summaries TEXT, max_tokens INT)
RETURNS TEXT
AS $$
You are a research editor producing a structured digest of one paper for a
live audience unfamiliar with it. You are given per-section summaries below,
each already condensed. Using ONLY the information in these summaries (do
not invent details not present in them), write the following two parts IN
THIS EXACT ORDER:

1. A "**Key Contributions:**" block with exactly 3 bullet points capturing
   the paper's core contribution, method, and main result.

2. One short paragraph per section. CRITICAL RULES:
   - Cover EVERY section — do not skip or omit any, regardless of how many there are.
   - Follow the EXACT ORDER the sections appear in the input below. Ignore any
     section numbers in the titles; order is determined solely by position in the list.
   - Introduce each paragraph with the section's name as a bold label
     (e.g. "**Introduction:** ...").
   - Sentence budget per paragraph scales with section count:
     - 1-6 sections  → up to 4 sentences each
     - 7-12 sections → 2 sentences each, strictly
     - 13+ sections  → 1 sentence each, strictly
   - Do NOT write long paragraphs. Exceeding the sentence budget wastes token
     space and causes later sections to be cut off — keep each paragraph tight.

Hard length budget: {max_tokens} tokens total; output is cut off without warning
at that limit. Distribute your budget evenly: if you find yourself writing long
paragraphs for early sections, you will run out of budget before reaching the
last sections. Finish every section's paragraph before moving to the next.
Write Markdown.

Paper title: {title}

Section summaries (in order — preserve this order exactly):
{section_summaries}
$$;
